        {
          "order": 1,
          "kind": "passage",
          "title": "Charting a coastline",
          "body": "Long before satellites, the shape of a coastline was argued over for decades. A chart was a\nclaim, and rival nations published charts that disagreed by tens of kilometres. The disagreement\nmattered: a captain steering by a bad chart lost a ship, and an admiralty that admitted its charts\nwere wrong lost an argument about territory.\n\nThe remedy was slow and unglamorous. Survey ships spent seasons anchored off the same headland,\ntiming the same stars, so that one point on one island could be fixed with confidence. Everything\nelse was then measured relative to it by triangulation — a chain of angles carried inland from a\nbaseline that had been paced out, sometimes over frozen lakes because only ice was flat enough.\n\nWhat changed the profession was not a single instrument but a habit: publishing the error. A chart\nthat stated how far it might be wrong could be used carefully. A chart that claimed certainty could\nonly be believed or ignored, and both were dangerous.",
          "questions": [
            {
              "id": "r-1",
              "order": 1,
              "type": "true-false-notgiven",
              "prompt": "Rival nations sometimes published charts that disagreed with each other.",
              "answerKey": {
                "accepted": ["TRUE"]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "r-2",
              "order": 2,
              "type": "true-false-notgiven",
              "prompt": "A bad chart could cause a ship to be lost.",
              "answerKey": {
                "accepted": ["TRUE"]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "r-3",
              "order": 3,
              "type": "true-false-notgiven",
              "prompt": "Triangulation was invented by survey ships' captains.",
              "answerKey": {
                "accepted": ["NOT GIVEN"]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "r-4",
              "order": 4,
              "type": "true-false-notgiven",
              "prompt": "Baselines were never measured across frozen lakes.",
              "answerKey": {
